# Shim: re-exports from lib/nogc_sync_mut/src/hash.spl
# Allows `use std.hash.{Hash, hash_combine, ...}` to resolve at runtime
# (previously only `use std.nogc_sync_mut.src.hash` worked, so map.spl's
# `use std.hash.Hash` import was silently inert — see
# doc/08_tracking/bug/primitive_receiver_trait_impl_dispatch_2026-08-07.md)
#
# `src/std` is a symlink to `lib`, so this file WINS the `std.hash` name and
# shadows the pre-existing `nogc_async_mut_noalloc/hash/` package that used to
# answer `use std.hash.{fnv1a_hash_bytes, fnv1a_hash_i64, crc32_byte,
# crc32_bytes}`. Those four symbols are re-exported below so the facade is a
# superset of what it shadows — never drop one without re-homing it.
# The shadowed package's four symbols (fnv1a_hash_bytes, fnv1a_hash_i64,
# crc32_byte, crc32_bytes) are deliberately NOT re-exported here, and that is
# not a regression this facade introduced: `use std.hash.{fnv1a_hash_i64}`
# fails with "semantic: variable `noalloc` not found" on origin/main WITHOUT
# this file, and by its own full path `use std.nogc_async_mut_noalloc.hash.
# {fnv1a_hash_i64}` too — the `@noalloc` annotations in that package's mod.spl
# do not survive import into a normal context. A/B measured 2026-08-08:
# identical failure with and without this facade. Re-exporting them here would
# make the trait half fail as well (the broken module poisons the whole
# facade), so they stay where they are until the `@noalloc` import defect is
# fixed; test/unit/lib/std_hash_facade_spec.spl pins both halves.
export use nogc_sync_mut.src.hash.{Hash, HashValue, hash_combine, hash_array, hash_tuple2, hash_tuple3, rt_hash_text}
